  <section class="hero-section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="overline">Mahr &amp; Dower · UAE 2026</p>
      <h1>Mahr in UAE Divorce: Prompt, Deferred &amp; Unpaid Dower</h1>
      <p class="byline">By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant · Updated November 2026</p>
      <p class="hero-sub">
        Mahr is not a gift your husband can choose to ignore. It is the wife’s property under the marriage contract (Article 45 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024). Whether part of it was paid at the wedding or all of it was deferred, any unpaid amount falls due on irrevocable separation or death (Article 46), and a court judgment for it is enforced like other money judgments.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-meta">
        <span>Updated: November 2026</span>
        <span class="sep">·</span>
        <span>By Aleksi Suon, UAE Expat &amp; Legal Consultant</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <main class="main-content">
    <div class="container">

      <div class="summary-box">
        <h2>At a Glance: Mahr in a UAE Divorce</h2>
        <div class="summary-grid">
          <div class="summary-card">
            <div class="summary-label">Deferred mahr falls due</div>
            <div class="summary-amount">On irrevocable separation or death</div>
            <div class="summary-note">Article 46</div>
          </div>
          <div class="summary-card">
            <div class="summary-label">Divorce before consummation</div>
            <div class="summary-amount">Half the named mahr</div>
            <div class="summary-note">Article 47(3)</div>
          </div>
          <div class="summary-card">
            <div class="summary-label">Dubai court fee</div>
            <div class="summary-amount">AED 100 per application</div>
            <div class="summary-note">Capped at AED 2,000 (Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015)</div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What Is Mahr and Why Does It Matter in UAE Divorce?</h2>
        <p>
          Mahr (the dowry, or dower) is property the husband pays to the wife under the marriage contract, and it is her property (Article 45 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024). It is obligatory by the contract unless the contract is void (Article 47). Unlike a dowry in many cultural traditions, where the bride's family pays the groom, in Islam the husband pays the wife. This distinction matters legally: mahr belongs to the wife, not to her family, her husband or anyone else.
        </p>
        <p>
          Since 15 April 2025 the governing statute for Muslim family matters is <a href="/en/uae-divorce-law/">Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024 on Personal Status</a>, which replaced Federal Law No. 28 of 2005. Under the new law mahr remains a core financial right of the wife, and the nikah (marriage) contract is the primary evidence of the agreed amount.
        </p>
        <p>
          The 2024 law also sets documentation duties. The marriage contract must be documented in court (Article 41), and a husband must document a divorce before the competent court within 15 days of its occurrence (Article 58). Good court records make a mahr claim easier to prove, because the agreed figure and the divorce date are both on file.
        </p>
        <div class="info-box info-box--info">
          <h3>Key point on ownership</h3>
          <p>Once mahr is paid it belongs entirely to the wife (Article 45). She can spend it, save it, invest it, or give it away. It sits separate from any <a href="/en/property-division-divorce-uae/">property division</a> or maintenance question.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>The Two Types of Mahr</h2>
        <p>
          The spouses may agree to defer all or part of the mahr (Article 46), so a contract can name a prompt part paid at the wedding, a deferred part held back as protection for the wife, or both. The Arabic terms are used in marriage contracts across the region, so it helps to know them.
        </p>
        <div class="table-wrapper">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th>Type</th>
                <th>Arabic term</th>
                <th>When it is payable</th>
                <th>What the law says</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td class="td-highlight">Prompt mahr</td>
                <td>Muqaddam (مقدم) or mu'ajjal (معجل)</td>
                <td>At the marriage, or on request if no deferral or time was agreed</td>
                <td>Due on request if not deferred (Article 46); she may refuse to move into the marital home until she receives her due dowry (Article 50)</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td class="td-highlight">Deferred mahr</td>
                <td>Mu'akhkhar (مؤخر) or mu'ajjal (مؤجل)</td>
                <td>At the agreed time, and in all cases on irrevocable separation or death</td>
                <td>Falls due on irrevocable separation or the death of either spouse (Article 46)</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <h3>Prompt Mahr (Muqaddam)</h3>
        <p>
          Prompt mahr is meant to be paid at the time of marriage or soon after, and it passes straight into the wife's ownership. If no deferral and no time were agreed, it is due on request (Article 46). If the husband never paid it, the wife can claim it, including during divorce: where the marriage was consummated before she received her due dowry, it stays a debt she can claim at any time (Article 50).
        </p>
        <h3>Deferred Mahr (Mu'akhkhar)</h3>
        <p>
          Deferred mahr is agreed at the marriage but payable later, at the time the spouses agreed, and in all cases on irrevocable separation or the death of either spouse (Article 46). It is the part of mahr that works as financial security for the wife. Because it is written into the nikah contract, it is a fixed debt waiting to fall due, not a discretionary payment the court decides at the end of the marriage.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How Mahr Is Recorded and How the Amount Is Set</h2>
        <p>
          Mahr is written into the nikah contract at the time of marriage. The contract normally states a single figure or splits it into prompt and deferred parts. A valid contract needs two witnesses (Article 26), and it must be documented in court (Article 41), so the mahr amount becomes part of the official record. That written figure is what a court enforces later; verbal side promises are hard to prove.
        </p>
        <p>
          The amount is the one the contract names. Where no dowry was named, it was denied in the contract or it was not specified correctly, the court applies the usual dowry, mahr al-mithl (Article 48). Amounts vary widely from family to family, so this guide does not quote typical figures.
        </p>
        <div class="info-box info-box--info">
          <h3>Get the number written down</h3>
          <p>Whatever you agree, make sure the exact mahr figure and the split between prompt and deferred are stated in the registered nikah contract. A clear written amount removes most room for later dispute and is the most useful document in a mahr claim.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>What Happens to Each Type of Mahr at Divorce?</h2>
        <p>
          The timing of a mahr claim depends on which type is outstanding when the divorce happens.
        </p>
        <h3>Unpaid prompt mahr</h3>
        <p>
          If the prompt mahr was never handed over, it remains a debt the wife can claim during divorce proceedings, whether the divorce is a <a href="/en/talaq-uae/">talaq</a> by the husband or a court-ordered judicial divorce. A <a href="/en/khula-divorce-uae/">khula</a> is different: when the dowry is the consideration, the wife returns what she received and the unpaid balance is dropped (Article 66). If no deferral or time was agreed, the prompt mahr was due on request (Article 46).
        </p>
        <h3>Deferred mahr</h3>
        <p>
          Deferred mahr falls due on irrevocable separation or on the death of either spouse (Article 46 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024). After a final divorce judgment, a khula or a third talaq, that is straight away; after a first or second talaq it is once the iddah ends without the husband taking his wife back, because a revocable divorce does not end the marriage contract until then (Article 59). From that point the whole deferred amount is a debt owed by the ex-husband, and the court decides how it is paid if he does not pay it voluntarily.
        </p>
        <h3>Divorce before consummation</h3>
        <p>
          If the husband divorces before intercourse or valid seclusion, the wife is owed half the named mahr, or no more than half the usual mahr if none was named (Article 47(3)), and valid seclusion confirms the full amount (Article 47(2)). Our guide to <a href="/en/divorce-before-consummation-uae/">divorce before consummation</a> works through the AED sums.
        </p>
        <div class="info-box info-box--warning">
          <h3>Claim mahr during the divorce, not after</h3>
          <p>Combining the mahr claim with the main divorce case is usually simpler than a separate claim later, which means re-engaging lawyers and opening a fresh file. Fold mahr into your <a href="/en/wife-rights-after-divorce-uae/">wider financial claims</a> from the start.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Disputes Over the Amount: Unnamed Mahr and Proof of Payment</h2>
        <p>
          Where no amount was validly named, the wife does not lose her right. The court applies the usual dowry, mahr al-mithl (Article 48), and if the divorce comes before intercourse or valid seclusion she is entitled to no more than half of it (Article 47(3)).
        </p>
        <p>
          Disputes also arise when the husband says he already paid. The court looks at the evidence of payment, so both spouses should keep bank transfers, receipts or written acknowledgments.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>The amount named in the contract is the starting point (Articles 45 and 47)</li>
          <li>If no amount was validly named, the usual dowry applies (Article 48)</li>
          <li>The full named mahr is confirmed by intercourse, valid seclusion or the death of either spouse (Article 47(2))</li>
          <li>Unpaid due mahr stays a debt the wife can claim (Article 50)</li>
          <li>Keep proof of every payment, whichever side you are on</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>How to Claim Unpaid Mahr at the Personal Status Court</h2>
        <p>
          Claiming unpaid mahr is a straightforward process if you have your nikah contract and can confirm the agreed amount. The steps below apply to both prompt and deferred mahr under the <a href="/en/sharia-divorce-uae/">Sharia-track personal status</a> system.
        </p>
        <div class="step-list">
          <div class="step-item">
            <div class="step-num">1</div>
            <div class="step-content">
              <h3>Gather your nikah contract and marriage documents</h3>
              <p>The original nikah contract (or a certified copy) is your primary evidence. It should state the mahr amount explicitly. If you married abroad, the document needs to be legalised for use in UAE courts: authenticated in the issuing country, legalised by the UAE Embassy there and attested by the UAE Ministry of Foreign Affairs, then translated into Arabic. An apostille alone is not enough, because the UAE is not a party to the Apostille Convention.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step-item">
            <div class="step-num">2</div>
            <div class="step-content">
              <h3>Decide who files, and where</h3>
              <p>You may appear in person or through a lawyer (Article 52 of Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022), but only a UAE-licensed lawyer may give legal advice and represent you (Article 6 of Federal Decree-Law No. 34 of 2022). A wife’s dowry claim can be filed at the court where she or the husband lives or works, or where the marital home is (Article 3 of Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2024). In Dubai this is the Dubai Courts Personal Status Court; in Abu Dhabi, the Abu Dhabi Judicial Department.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step-item">
            <div class="step-num">3</div>
            <div class="step-content">
              <h3>File the claim and pay the court fee</h3>
              <p>In Dubai the personal status fee is AED 100 per application, capped at AED 2,000 (Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015); in other emirates, confirm the fee when you register, and pay it within 3 business days of the day after the payment notice or the registration is void (Article 47 of Federal Decree-Law No. 42 of 2022). The claim goes in with the nikah contract, ID documents and the divorce judgment if it has been issued. The supervising judge may first refer the spouses to Family Guidance (Article 8), and in Dubai a personal status claim is filed with the Family Conciliation referral.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step-item">
            <div class="step-num">4</div>
            <div class="step-content">
              <h3>Attend the hearing</h3>
              <p>The judge examines the nikah contract, confirms the agreed mahr, and checks what portion (if any) has been paid. The husband may produce proof of payment. The court then rules on what is owed.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="step-item">
            <div class="step-num">5</div>
            <div class="step-content">
              <h3>Receive the judgment ordering payment</h3>
              <p>The judgment orders payment of what is owed. If he pays, the matter is closed. If he does not, you open an enforcement file; in Dubai the fee for that is AED 50 (Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015).</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Enforcing Mahr as a Debt Through the Execution Court</h2>
        <p>
          A court judgment for unpaid mahr is an enforceable order. It is enforced as a money judgment, and enforcement can reach the debtor's bank balances, salary and other assets, including property registered in his name. A travel ban can also be requested; in Dubai the fee for a travel ban or order on petition is AED 100 (Dubai Law No. 21 of 2015). Which measures the court orders, and how quickly, depends on the case and on what assets can be found.
        </p>
        <p>
          One distinction matters. Article 98 makes continuous alimony due from the filing date a privileged debt that ranks ahead of other debts, while past alimony is an ordinary debt. That priority covers continuous alimony; it does not extend to mahr. For how enforcement files work in practice, see our guides to <a href="/en/unpaid-alimony-enforcement-uae/">enforcing unpaid alimony</a> and <a href="/en/travel-ban-divorce-uae/">travel bans during divorce</a>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Mahr in Khula Divorce</h2>
        <p>
          Khula is separation at the wife's request with the husband's acceptance of a consideration, and it takes effect as a minor irrevocable divorce (Article 65). The consideration may be any property. If it is the dowry, surrendering what she received is enough and the rest of the dowry is dropped, and the consideration may not waive the children's rights, maintenance or custody. If the husband stubbornly refuses, the court orders khula for an appropriate consideration (Article 66). The spouses document the khula before the court within 15 days (Article 68), and there is no iddah maintenance after a khula (Article 101).
        </p>
        <p>
          Because a large deferred mahr can be given up this way, take advice before choosing khula over other routes. Before intercourse or valid seclusion there is another route: if the wife asks for revocation and the husband will neither divorce her nor agree khula, the court revokes the contract if she returns the dowry she received and reconciliation is impossible, without her having to prove harm (Article 75). Our dedicated <a href="/en/khula-divorce-uae/">khula divorce guide</a> walks through the trade-offs, and the <a href="/en/husband-rights-divorce-uae/">husband's rights guide</a> explains his side of the same bargain.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Mahr on the Husband's Death</h2>
        <p>
          Deferred mahr falls due on death as well as on divorce: Article 46 makes the dowry due on the death of either spouse. If the husband dies before paying it, the widow claims the unpaid mahr from his estate rather than from a living ex-husband.
        </p>
        <p>
          Act promptly, and raise the claim before the estate is distributed. Keep the nikah contract safe, because it is the same core evidence used in a divorce-based claim.
        </p>
        <div class="info-box info-box--info">
          <h3>Unpaid mahr stays a debt</h3>
          <p>Article 50 says that where the marriage was consummated before the wife received her due dowry, it stays a debt she can claim at any time. Keep the nikah contract and any record of payments, whatever happens later.</p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Mahr vs Alimony vs Mut'a: Three Separate Rights</h2>
        <p>
          Many women do not realise that mahr, alimony (nafaqa) and mut'a are three separate entitlements. Claiming one does not cancel the others, but each has its own conditions.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Mahr:</strong> the dowry fixed by the nikah contract. It is the wife's property, and any unpaid part falls due on irrevocable separation or death (Articles 45 and 46).</li>
          <li><strong>Alimony / Nafaqa:</strong> maintenance covers food, clothing, housing, medical treatment and education according to custom (Article 95), and the court estimates it from the payer's means and the recipient's condition (Article 96). After a divorce, iddah maintenance is due after a revocable divorce, after an irrevocable divorce only if she is pregnant, and not after a khula (Article 101). See our guides to the <a href="/en/iddah-waiting-period-uae/">iddah waiting period</a> and to <a href="/en/alimony-uae/">alimony</a>.</li>
          <li><strong>Mut'a (compensation):</strong> due where the husband divorces a wife in a consummated valid marriage by his sole will, without her request or a cause from her, on top of iddah maintenance. It is set according to his situation, cannot exceed one year's maintenance for women in her position, and may be paid in instalments (Article 102).</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          All three can be claimed in a single proceeding. A well-drafted petition packages the mahr claim with the other financial claims, which cuts down court appearances and stops the husband arguing that settling one claim satisfied the rest.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <h2>Mahr and Non-Muslims Under the Civil Law</h2>
        <p>
          Mahr is a feature of the Islamic nikah contract, so it arises in Muslim marriages. Non-Muslims who divorce in the UAE under <a href="/en/non-muslim-divorce-uae/">Federal Decree-Law No. 41 of 2022 on Civil Personal Status</a>, in force since 1 February 2023, use a no-fault divorce in which either spouse's wish to separate is enough (Article 7), and the money questions after the divorce run through alimony and compensation instead.
        </p>
        <p>
          A non-Muslim wife's financial protection comes through alimony decided on application after the divorce judgment, using factors such as the length of the marriage, the wife's age, each spouse's finances and any fault or harm (Article 9), and through lump-sum compensation the court can award at its discretion if the spouses did not agree, with criteria that include contributions to the other spouse's wealth (Article 6 of Cabinet Resolution No. 122 of 2023). The civil law applies unless one spouse holds to the law of their home country (Article 1), which can change the financial picture again.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="content-section">
        <FAQSection items={faqItems} lang="en" />
      </section>

      <div class="cta-box">
        <h2>Get Expert Advice on Your Mahr Claim</h2>
        <p>Every mahr situation is different. The amount, the payment history, the type of divorce, and whether children are involved all affect your strategy. A short consultation gives you clarity on your options and next steps.</p>
        <LeadForm source="mahr-divorce-uae" lang="en" variant="inline" />
      </div>

